WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:03.197
I was getting on in the first year of my apprenticeship. Because I was growing too

2
00:00:03.198 --> 00:00:07.793
old for the village school, my education came to an end. But not before Biddy had

3
00:00:07.794 --> 00:00:10.697
taught me everything she knew. Whatever she taught me, I tried to pass on to Joe.

4
00:00:10.698 --> 00:00:16.689
But this makes me sound like a better person than I was. What I really wanted was

5
00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:21.277
to make Joe less ignorant so that Estella wouldn’t scorn him. Our place of study was

6
00:00:21.278 --> 00:00:29.356
out on the marshes. I never knew Joe to remember anything from one Sunday to the

7
00:00:29.357 --> 00:00:33.594
next or to learn anything from my teaching. But it was pleasant and quiet to sit

8
00:00:33.595 --> 00:00:37.666
there and watch the ships sailing on the river. As so often happened, I began to

9
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:40.840
think of Estella and Miss Havisham. "Joe, don’t you think I should pay Miss Havisham a

10
00:00:40.841 --> 00:00:46.753
visit?" "She might think you wanted something from her, Pip. And she told me you should

11
00:00:46.754 --> 00:00:54.245
not expect any more from her." "I only meant that, since we aren’t busy right now, if

12
00:00:54.246 --> 00:01:00.173
you would give me a half-day off tomorrow, I could go uptown and call on Miss

13
00:01:00.174 --> 00:01:04.403
Est—Havisham." Joe agreed to the visit. "But if she does not receive you cordially, there will

14
00:01:04.404 --> 00:01:08.736
be no future visits." Now Joe had a worker at his forge named Orlick who had

15
00:01:08.737 --> 00:01:14.728
always disliked me. When I was very small and timid, he told me the devil lived

16
00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:18.485
in a dark corner of the forge. He also told me that once every seven years,

17
00:01:18.486 --> 00:01:23.405
it was necessary to make the fire using a live boy for fuel! When I became Joe’s

18
00:01:23.406 --> 00:01:27.215
apprentice, Orlick disliked me even more. I knew it was because he suspected that I would

19
00:01:27.216 --> 00:01:35.564
one day take over his position at the forge. Orlick was at work the next day

20
00:01:35.565 --> 00:01:38.636
when I reminded Joe of my afternoon off. "Now, master!" he said to Joe. "Surely you’re

21
00:01:38.637 --> 00:01:43.215
not going to favor one of us. If young Pip has a holiday, do the same

22
00:01:43.216 --> 00:01:46.620
for me." "What would you do with your time off?" asked Joe. "What’s Pip going to

23
00:01:46.621 --> 00:01:50.215
do?" "Pip’s going uptown," said Joe. "Well, two people can go uptown!" growled Orlick. Joe refused

24
00:01:50.216 --> 00:01:53.707
to hear any more until Orlick was in a better temper. Orlick dove at the furnace and

25
00:01:53.708 --> 00:01:58.366
pulled out a red-hot bar of metal. Then he waved it about my head, laid it

26
00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:04.207
on the anvil, and started hammering furiously. After a while Joe said to Orlick, "Since you

27
00:02:04.208 --> 00:02:08.858
generally get your work done, let it be a half-day for all." Meanwhile my sister had

28
00:02:08.859 --> 00:02:15.191
been standing in the yard, listening and looking in through the window. "You must be a

29
00:02:15.192 --> 00:02:22.017
rich man to waste wages that way," she said to Joe. "If I were his master

30
00:02:22.018 --> 00:02:30.169
. . ." "You’d be everybody’s master if you could," said Orlick. "Leave her alone," said

31
00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.994
Joe quietly. "As a master I’d have no problem with rogues," said my sister. "Including you—the worst

32
00:02:32.995 --> 00:02:35.818
rogue between here and France." "You’re a foul shrew," shouted Orlick. "What did you say?" screamed

33
00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:38.993
my sister. "I tell you, leave her alone," said Joe. And with that he and Orlick

34
00:02:38.994 --> 00:02:47.826
began to fight. But no one could stand up to Joe, and Orlick soon found himself

35
00:02:47.827 --> 00:02:51.239
lying on the ground like the pale young gentleman. I went upstairs to get dressed for

36
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.810
my visit to Miss Havisham’s. When I left for Miss Havisham’s, Joe and Orlick were sweeping

37
00:02:55.811 --> 00:03:02.818
up as if nothing had happened. Miss Havisham was alone in her room. "I hope you want

38
00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:10.143
nothing," she said briskly, "because you’ll get nothing." "I only wanted you to know that I’m

39
00:03:10.144 --> 00:03:17.540
doing well in my apprenticeship and I’m much obliged to you." I looked around the gloomy

40
00:03:17.541 --> 00:03:22.119
room. She seemed to soften. "You may come to visit me now and then. Come on

41
00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:27.469
your birthday. Hey!" She peered intently at me. "You’re looking around for Estella!" Indeed I had

42
00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:40.032
been looking for Estella. "I . . . I . . . hope that she’s well."

43
00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:44.604
"Estella has gone abroad to be educated. I hear she’s prettier than ever and admired by

44
00:03:44.605 --> 00:03:48.111
all who see her. Do you feel you’ve lost her?" she said with a disagreeable laugh. There

45
00:03:48.112 --> 00:03:55.516
was such malicious enjoyment in that last question that I didn’t know what to say. Miss

46
00:03:55.517 --> 00:04:02.238
Havisham spared me the trouble of answering by dismissing me. I went downstairs and left the

47
00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:07.428
house. As the gate closed behind me, I felt even more dissatisfied with my home and

48
00:04:07.429 --> 00:04:10.587
my trade. I wandered along the street, looking unhappily through shop windows and thinking about what

49
00:04:10.588 --> 00:04:16.500
I would buy if I were a gentleman. Mr. Wopsle came out of the bookshop and

50
00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:24.079
urged me to come along with him to Mr. Pumblechook’s for tea. I knew I would

51
00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.246
be miserable at home, so I went with him. Mr. Wopsle read a new book aloud in

52
00:04:28.247 --> 00:04:31.079
a quite dramatic fashion, and we stayed until nine thirty that night. It was very dark

53
00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.150
when we set out for home. We were just commenting on the heavy mist when we

54
00:04:33.151 --> 00:04:34.658
came upon a man slouching against a tree. "Hello!" said Mr. Wopsle. "Is that you, Orlick?"

55
00:04:34.659 --> 00:04:39.730
"Yes," he said, coming over to us. "You’re late," I said. "So are you." "We have

56
00:04:39.731 --> 00:04:46.555
been engaging," Mr. Wopsle said grandly, "in an intellectual evening." Orlick was not impressed. "The guns

57
00:04:46.556 --> 00:04:49.127
are firing again," he said as he fell into step alongside us. "From the prison ships?"

58
00:04:49.128 --> 00:04:51.468
I asked. "Yes. You’ll hear one soon." And sure enough we had not walked much farther when

59
00:04:51.469 --> 00:04:56.889
I heard the familiar booming sound deadened by the mist. "It’s a good night for running

60
00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:00.048
off," said Orlick. After a while we came to the village. As we passed the pub,

61
00:05:00.049 --> 00:05:07.548
we were surprised to find it in a state of commotion with the door wide open.

62
00:05:07.549 --> 00:05:13.294
"They must have captured the convict," said Mr. Wopsle. "I’ll go in and find out what

63
00:05:13.295 --> 00:05:16.873
happened." He was only inside for a moment before he came running out again. "There’s something

64
00:05:16.874 --> 00:05:20.524
wrong up at your place, Pip! Let’s go." "What is it?" I asked, running to keep up

65
00:05:20.525 --> 00:05:26.548
with him. Orlick also ran at my side. "It seems that the house was broken into

66
00:05:26.549 --> 00:05:34.937
when Joe was out," said Mr. Wopsle. "By convicts, I suppose. Somebody has been attacked and

67
00:05:34.938 --> 00:05:39.945
hurt." We were running too fast to talk anymore, and we didn’t stop running until we

68
00:05:39.946 --> 00:05:42.667
got to our kitchen. The whole village was either there or in the yard. A surgeon

69
00:05:42.668 --> 00:05:47.358
and Joe and a group of women were all on the floor in the middle of

70
00:05:47.359 --> 00:05:52.254
the kitchen. They stepped back when they saw me, and so I saw my sister lying

71
00:05:52.255 --> 00:05:55.747
unconscious on the floor. She had been knocked down by a tremendous blow to the back of

72
00:05:55.748 --> 00:05:58.739
her head. Nothing had been taken from any part of the house. But there was one

73
00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:02.007
remarkable piece of evidence on the spot. She had been struck by something blunt and heavy.

74
00:06:02.008 --> 00:06:09.746
And on the floor beside her was a convict’s leg iron that had been filed open.

